          <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ink mb-4 dark:text-on-primary">Pakistan Capital Gain Tax Guide: Tax on Property and Share Gains</h1>
          <p class="text-lg text-body dark:text-muted">Last updated: July 2026 | 11 min read</p>

            <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551288049-bebda4e38f71?w=1200&q=80" alt="Tax documents and calculator on desk" class="mb-6 w-full rounded-lg object-cover max-h-80 sm:max-h-96" loading="lazy" />

            <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-body dark:text-muted">
            <p>Capital gains tax (CGT) in Pakistan applies to profits from selling assets such as immovable property, listed shares, securities, and mutual funds. The rules differ significantly depending on the type of asset, how long you held it, and whether you are a registered filer on the FBR Active Taxpayer List. For property investors and share traders, understanding CGT rates, holding period relief, and the distinction between filers and non-filers can save tens of thousands of rupees per transaction.</p>

            <p>This guide explains capital gains tax on property and listed shares in Pakistan, holds the latest rates for FY 2024/25, and shows how to calculate your gain. You can also use our <a href="/countries/pakistan/capital-gains/capital-gains-tax-calculator">capital gain tax calculator Pakistan</a> for precise estimates.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Capital Gains Tax on Property</h2>
            <p>Under Section 37 of the Income Tax Ordinance 2001, capital gains tax on immovable property is levied on the seller at the time of disposal. The gain is calculated as the sale price (or FBR valuation, whichever is higher) minus the purchase price, minus allowable improvement costs and brokerage fees.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">CGT Rates for Properties Acquired Before July 1, 2024</h3>
            <p>For properties purchased before July 1, 2024, CGT depends on the holding period. The longer you hold, the lower your rate:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Up to 1 year:</strong> 15% (filer) / 45% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>1–2 years:</strong> 12.5% (filer) / 37.5% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>2–3 years:</strong> 10% (filer) / 30% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>3–4 years:</strong> 7.5% (filer) / 22.5% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>4–5 years:</strong> 5% (filer) / 15% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>5–6 years:</strong> 2.5% (filer) / 7.5% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>More than 6 years:</strong> 0% — fully exempt</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Non-filers pay three times the filer rate, which is why ATL registration is so valuable for real estate investors. You can verify your ATL status on <a href="https://iris.fbr.gov.pk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">iris.fbr.gov.pk</a>.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">CGT for Properties Acquired On or After July 1, 2024</h3>
            <p>The Finance Act 2024 introduced a major reform: for properties acquired on or after July 1, 2024, the holding period concept was eliminated for Active Taxpayer List filers. These properties are now subject to a <strong>flat 15% CGT</strong> regardless of how long they are held. Non-filers face much steeper rates, effectively barring informal sector participants from property investment.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Capital Gains Tax on Listed Shares</h2>
            <p>Listed shares, securities, and mutual fund units are taxed under Section 37A. The tax rate depends on how long you held the shares before selling:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Less than 1 year:</strong> 15% (filer) / 20% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>1 to 2 years:</strong> 12.5% (filer) / 17.5% (non-filer)</li>
              <li><strong>More than 2 years:</strong> 0% (filer) / 5% (non-filer)</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Section 37A does not apply to shares sold outside the registered stock exchange or not settled through NCCPL. For typical retail investors trading on the Pakistan Stock Exchange (PSX), these are the rates that matter.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How to Calculate Capital Gains Tax</h2>
            <p>Calculating CGT involves three steps:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li><strong>Determine the gain:</strong> Sale price (or FBR fair market value, whichever is higher) minus cost of acquisition plus improvement costs.</li>
              <li><strong>Identify your holding period:</strong> Count from the date of purchase to the date of sale.</li>
              <li><strong>Apply the rate:</strong> Multiply the net gain by the applicable CGT rate based on asset type, holding period, and filer status.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>For example, if you bought a plot for PKR 5 million and sold it two years later for PKR 8 million, your capital gain is PKR 3 million. At the 2–3 year filer rate of 10%, your CGT is PKR 300,000. If you were a non-filer, the rate is 30%, making your CGT PKR 900,000 — a difference of PKR 600,000. Use our <a href="/countries/pakistan/capital-gains/capital-gains-tax-calculator">capital gain tax calculator Pakistan</a> to automate this calculation.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Capital Losses</h2>
            <p>If you sell an asset at a loss, you can carry the capital loss forward to offset against future capital gains. However, capital losses cannot be set off against ordinary income such as salary or business profits. You must report the loss in your annual income tax return to claim the carryforward benefit.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Conclusion</h2>
            <p>Capital gains tax in Pakistan is one area where the difference between a filer and a non-filer is staggering. By maintaining your ATL status, you can slash property CGT from 45% to 15% on short-term sales and enjoy 0% tax on listed shares held for more than two years. Plan your disposals carefully, document your holding periods, and use our <a href="/countries/pakistan/capital-gains/capital-gains-tax-calculator">capital gain tax calculator Pakistan</a> to model your transaction before selling.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the capital gains tax rate on property in Pakistan?</h3>
            <p>For properties acquired before July 1, 2024, CGT ranges from 15% (short-term, filer) to 0% (held more than 6 years, filer). Non-filers pay 3x these rates. For properties acquired after July 1, 2024, CGT is flat 15% for ATL filers on all holding periods.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How do you calculate capital gains tax on a property sale?</h3>
            <p>Calculate the net gain by subtracting the purchase price, improvement costs, and brokerage fees from the sale price. Multiply the result by the applicable CGT rate based on holding period and filer status.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">What is the holding period for capital gains tax on shares?</h3>
            <p>Shares held for less than 1 year are taxed at 15% for filers. Shares held for 1 to 2 years are taxed at 12.5%. Shares held for more than 2 years are exempt for filers but taxed at 5% for non-filers.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Can I set off capital losses against my salary income?</h3>
            <p>No. Capital losses can only be carried forward to offset future capital gains. They cannot reduce your taxable salary or business income.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Do non-filers pay higher capital gains tax?</h3>
            <p>Yes. Non-filers pay significantly higher CGT rates, often three times the filer rate. For example, on a property sold within 1 year, non-filers pay 45% while filers pay 15%. Being on the ATL is the most effective way to reduce CGT.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Is capital gains tax on property the same as withholding tax?</h3>
            <p>No. Capital gains tax is the final tax on your profit reported in your annual return. Withholding tax under Sections 236C and 236K is an advance tax deducted at the time of sale or purchase and credited against your final CGT or income tax liability.</p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How can I avoid capital gains tax on property?</h3>
            <p>Complete exemption applies if you hold property for more than 6 years (for pre-July 2024 acquisitions). For newer acquisitions, the 15% flat rate for filers is the minimum. You can minimize the overall burden by claiming deductions for improvement costs and ensuring correct valuation.</p>
